    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 091908 CCA\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1205 PM PDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  The warming trend peaks on Thursday when the Extreme Heat Warning \n   goes into effect. Temperatures cool into and through the weekend.\n\n*  Spotty storm activity will be possible in the Sierra Nevada and \n   higher elevations of eastern San Bernardino today. \n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts expected across the region \n   Friday through the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Tuesday\n\nA strong high pressure continues to build into the region leading to \nmostly clear skies and warming temperatures. Afternoon highs look to \npeak around 5 degrees above average with most of the desert valleys \nranging from the upper 90s to around 105 degrees. Areas to the south \nof Lake Havasu and around Death Valley will exceed 110. The warmth \nwill also be felt with well above average overnight lows, around 10 \ndegrees average. This warm overnight combined with even stronger \nwarming for Thursday afternoon increases overall HeatRisk. As such, \nan Extreme Heat Warning has been issued for Thursday and covers \nDeath Valley, the Las Vegas Valley, Pahrump Valley, Moapa Valley, \nColorado River Valley, southern Mohave County, and southeastern San \nBernardino County. The upper level pattern begins to push a trough \ninto the area from the Northwest, pushing the high pressure east, \nand allowing for a cooling trend starting Friday. \n\nIn spite of the strong high pressure, there has been some weak \nconvection in the southern portions of the CWA with the potential \nfor some spotty activity in the Sierra Nevada near Bishop. Radar \nreturns have looked fairly week, and local gauges picking up very \nlittle, so these showers look to not be particularly impactful. \nAdditional chances will arrive to these areas tomorrow afternoon, \nbut again don't look to be impactful.\n\nThe late week pattern change will increase the pressure gradient \nfrom the high pressure to the east to the building trough to the \nnorthwest and result in increasing winds. Friday afternoon will \noffer breezy to gusty winds that look to ease slightly overnight but \nbuild further in into Saturday with widespread gusts above 30 mph \npossible and above 40 mph possible in higher terrain and favored \ngaps and passes. Winds look to reduce for Sunday but remain breezy. \nLonger term models are starting to favor breezy winds peaking around \n25 mph each afternoon into the next work week.\n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill generally follow typical daily directional patterns, with \neasterly to northeasterly winds through the afternoon before turning \nsouthwesterly after sunset. Wind speeds are expected to remain below \n10 knots through Thursday morning before turning to the southwest \ntomorrow afternoon and increasing to 10 to 15 knots. VFR conditions \nare expected through Thursday with FEW to SCT mid- and high-level \nclouds moving across the region. Temperatures will exceed 100 \ndegrees between 20Z and 02Z this afternoon and between 19Z and 03Z \nThursday.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds will follow \ntypical daily directional patterns through Thursday morning, with \nmost locations seeing speeds remain 10 knots or less. The exception \nto this will be in the lower Colorado River Valley, where gusty \nnortherly winds of 20 to 25 knots are expected at KIFP this \nafternoon. VFR conditions are expected regionwide through Thursday \nwith FEW to SCT mid- and high-level clouds moving across the area.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
